var e=`# CHAPTER 1 — Elizabeth

You know me as Nina.

I'm going to tell you something that neither Kim nor Jack knows. A little of my past. Maybe one day they'll find out. Not today.

---

I know almost nothing about my life before I was three.

At three, I was adopted. By a couple — a man and a woman who wanted me.

I never met my biological parents and I have no interest in meeting them. To me, a father and a mother are the people who chose me. Who cared. Who wanted me inside their lives. My dad and my mom.

Except I didn't have much luck with the second part.

---

My mother's name was Elizabeth.

Two years after adopting me, when I was five, she got sick. And she died.

I can't really remember her. I have flashes — a hand, a smell, the sound of a voice with no words. Everything I actually know about Elizabeth is what my dad told me, years later, once I was old enough to hear it.

But there's one thing that doesn't fade.

Every time I think about my mother, I feel something much bigger than the memory should be able to hold. It's not missing a person I knew. It's something else. Deeper.

I was loved for two years with so much strength, so much truth, so much wanting, that it got recorded somewhere a child's memory can't reach. It got stored in the body. Like muscle memory.

I don't remember my mother. But my body remembers being loved by her.

And that kept me standing through a lot of what came after.

---

The strongest flash I have is of sitting on a kitchen floor, leaning against a leg. The leg was hers. I don't see the face, I don't hear what she's saying, but I know it was a good day, because there's no fear anywhere inside that memory.

The rest comes from my dad.

He told me Elizabeth laughed loud, the kind of laugh that makes other people laugh along without knowing why. That she cried easily at movies and never in real life. That she had this habit of fixing other people's collars, even strangers', and nobody minded because it was her. That she said "no" with a calm that left no room for argument.

I heard all of that when I was already grown, and I recognized way too much. Not because I remembered. Because I was like that.

---

Once, when I was still little, I told my dad I wanted to see my mom.

He sent me everything he had. Her social media profile, which nobody ever deactivated. And a cloud folder full of videos and photos he never posted anywhere — kept just for himself.

But he showed me very little, and always one at a time, like someone rationing. I think he was afraid of using up what was left of her if he showed me everything at once.

In one of the videos she's holding me in her lap. I must have been two. Her hand closes on my shoulder every time I move. I watched that on loop for a long time, trying to turn the image into a memory. It never turned. But when I closed my eyes, my shoulder got warm.

Muscle memory. I told you.
`;export{e as default};
